<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  BRAND-01B — Android 12+ (API 31+) system splash screen, light mode.

  On Android 12+ the system splash is ALWAYS shown; the only question is what
  it is made of. With nothing declared, the platform cannot derive a colour
  from `windowBackground` (that is a layer-list, not a plain colour) and falls
  back to `?android:colorBackground` — white here, black in dark mode.

  Declared below: the warm brand cream, so the splash, the launch window and
  the Flutter first frame are all #F7F3EA and the handover is invisible.

  Deliberately NOT declared:
  - `windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon` — the platform default is the app's own
    launcher icon, which is already the approved BRAND-01A identity. Supplying
    a second bitmap would duplicate the emblem and let the two drift apart.
    The BRAND-01A adaptive foreground keeps all ink inside the 66dp safe
    circle, which is tighter than the splash icon's own mask, so the full
    composition survives here as well.
  - `windowSplashScreenIconBackgroundColor` — would draw a filled circle
    behind the emblem. The emblem sits directly on the cream instead.
  - `windowSplashScreenAnimationDuration` — the icon is static and the splash
    is dismissed by Flutter's first frame. No artificial delay is introduced.
  - `windowSplashScreenBrandingImage` — Android only shows it on some
    configurations and it is explicitly discouraged; no extra text or mark.

  These are platform attributes, so no androidx.core:core-splashscreen (or any
  other package) is added.

  This file only overrides LaunchTheme; NormalTheme continues to come from
  values/styles.xml.
-->
<resources>
    <style name="LaunchTheme" parent="@android:style/Theme.Light.NoTitleBar">
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@drawable/launch_background</item>
        <item name="android:windowSplashScreenBackground">@color/brand_launch_background</item>
    </style>
</resources>
